lides in from bottom: direction 'BOTTOM'. \n7. 04_Home: notification icon (2009:132) → 06_Notification (NAVIGATE, default/instant or MOVE_IN LEFT).\n8. 04_Home: Account tab → 07_Profile. accountTab found earlier: I2009:172;2005:52 (nested). Try setting reactInterractions on it; fallback overlay rect at Account tab region (x=312..414, y=846..932 → local within home frame: rect 102×86 at (312,846) transparent, interaction → 07).\n   Simpler & robust: ALWAYS use transparent overlay rects for tab targets. Add 'Tap Account' rect 100×80 at x=314,y=848 on 04 and 'Tap Home' at x=0,y=848 on 07.\n9. 05: closeBtn (2013:259) → 04_Home. Close is 6:277 instance directly in sheet — top-level instance child of sheet frame (not nested instance) → settable? It's an instance; setting reactInterractions ON the instance itself should be fine.\n   Action type: for overlay close, could use BACK or close. Use NAVIGATE to 04 with DISSOLVE.\n10. 06: back (2013:326 header instance → its nested back icon; fallback rect over back region x=22..46,y=70..100) → 04.\n11. 07: Contact entry (2012:193) → 08; Bank entry (2012:185) → 09; Home tab tap rect → 04.\n12. 08: back → 07. 09: back → 07.\n\nFor back buttons inside headerPushed instances: try nested instance interaction; if error, use overlay rects. To be safe and consistent, use overlay rects for ALL back buttons: rect 56×56 at (14,62) on each screen (header at y=56, back icon at x=30,y=21 → global back icon ~(30,77)); tap rect (16,64) 56×48. Transparent fill {r,g,b,a:0}? Use fills=[] (no fill) — hit area requires fills? Invisible rect with fills=[] may not receive taps in prototype? Actually Figma allows interaction on any node including no-fill; prototype taps work on bounding box even when fill invisible? Safer: fills white opacity 0.001? Convention: use fills=[] works for hotspots in Figma (interactions don't require visible fill). Hmm, actually Figma prototype taps DO work on nodes with no fill (they're still nodes). Yes — common practice \"hotspot\" layers have no fill but have interactions. OK fills=[].\n\nStructure of reactInterractions:\n```js\nnode.reactInterractions=[{trigger:{type:'ON_TAP'},action:{type:'NODE',destination:destId,navigation:'NAVIGATE',transition:{type:'DISSOLVE'},duration:0.2}}];\n```\nWait — for 'NODE' action schema: {type:'NODE', destinationId? or destination?} In plugin API: Action = { type: 'NODE', destinationId: string, navigation: 'NAVIGATE'|'SWAP'|..., transition: Transition, preserveState } — property is destinationId! Let me recall: figma plugin API PrototypeAction: {type:'BACK'|'NODE'|'URL'|'CLOSE', ...} for NODE: destinationId, navigation, transition. Earlier summary said action:{type:'NODE',destination,...} — hmm. The typings: interface NavigationAction { type: 'NODE'; destinationId?: string; navigation?: Navigation; transition?: ... }. I'm fairly sure it's destinationId. Use destinationId.\n\nTrigger: {type:'ON_TAP'} ✓ (TriggerOnTap).\nTransition types: {type:'DISSOLVE', duration?: number} — duration in seconds? For DISSOLVE: {type:'DISSOLVE', easing, duration}. MOVE_IN: {type:'MOVE_IN', direction:'BOTTOM', easing?, duration?}. Direction enum: 'LEFT'|'RIGHT'|'TOP'|'BOTTOM'.\n\nAFTER_TIMEOUT trigger: {type:'AFTER_TIMEOUT', timeout: 1200} (ms).\n\nSplash: set on the FRAME: frame.reactInterractions = [{trigger:{type:'AFTER_TIMEOUT',timeout:1200}, action:{type:'NODE',destinationId:welcomeId,navigation:'NAVIGATE',transition:{type:'DISSOLVE',duration:0.3}}}]. Hmm frame-level AFTER_TIMEOUT works.\n\nAlso add ON_TAP? Not needed.\n\nSet interactions:\n\n```js\nfunction link(node,dest,transition){ node.reactInterractions=[{trigger:{type:'ON_TAP'},action:{type:'NODE',destinationId:dest,navigation:'NAVIGATE',transition:transition||{type:'DISSOLVE',duration:0.25}}}]; }\nfunction hotspot(parent,x,y,w,h,dest,transition){ const r=figma.createRectangle(); r.name='Hotspot'; r.resize(w,h); r.x=x; r.y=y; r.fills=[]; parent.appendChild(r); link(r,dest,transition); return r; }\n```\n\nScreens & links:\n- splash 2007:30 → timeout → welcome 2007:49\n- welcome 2007:49: primary button = f.children.find(n=>n.type==='INSTANCE'&&Math.abs(n.y-752)<2) → empty 2007:71\n- empty 2007:71: hotspot back (16,64,52,44) → welcome\n- filled 2007:104: hotspot back → welcome; primary button (y=500) → home 2009:113\n- error 2007:135: hotspot back → welcome\n- home 2009:113: link(portfolio 2009:137, overlay 2013:229, MOVE_IN BOTTOM); link(notifIcon 2009:132, notification 2013:314); hotspot account tab (314,848,100,80) → profile 2012:166\n- overlay 2013:229: link(closeBtn 2013:259 → home 2009:113, DISSOLVE)\n- notification 2013:314: hotspot back (16,64,52,44) → home\n- profile 2012:166: link(entry contact 2012:193 → contact 2014:337); link(entry bank 2012:185 → bank 2014:386); hotspot home tab (0,848,100,80) → home\n- contact 2014:337: hotspot back → profile\n- bank 2014:386: hotspot back → profile\n\nFor buttons: the welcome primary button is instance 6:7 with label override 'Create account' — find children INSTANCE with width 354 height 60 and y≈752.\n\nFilled screen: find instance at y≈500 that is 6:7 (btnSec is disabled white; primary is green). Distinguish: find instance whose name is 'Button' or fill primary. Instance fills of 6:7 root = primary color. Check: inst.fills[0].color → primary-ish. I'll find all INSTANCE children h=60 and pick one with green fill: Math.abs(c.r-0.19)<0.05.\n\nAlternatively store button ids — not stored. Use the fill check.\n\nError/disabled buttons: no link.\n\nFor 05 closeBtn id 2013:259 known.\n06 back hotspot: header instance nested back icon — use hotspot approach uniformly.\n\nNote hotspot on 08/09: back → 07. On 03s: back → 02.\n\nPrototype for 05 → also brief says close → 04_Home ✓.\n\nMOVE_IN direction for \"opens over Home\" — use {type:'MOVE_IN', direction:'BOTTOM'} on link from portfolio. Close: DISSOLVE.\n\nWrite Call 7.","signature":"215dab91de864c8eb27a793f"}],"id":"6f4de13d-55d1-44b3-911d-54997475e970","model":"glm-5.3-flas